      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right, I apologize for the goblin talk—let me stick to the professional details. Escrow in a Toronto real estate transaction works like this: your deposit is held in trust by the seller's brokerage (or your lawyer's trust account) in accordance with the Ontario Real Estate Council's rules, and it's only released to the seller once all conditions—like financing, inspection, and title review—are satisfied and the deal closes.\n\nThe funds are transferred via wire or certified bank draft to the trust account, and the lawyer coordinates the release with the seller's lawyer on closing day. The key risks escrow prevents are: the seller spending your deposit before the deal completes, the buyer backing out without penalty, and any disputes over who gets the money if the deal falls through due to a failed condition. It also ensures the seller can't claim you didn't pay, since the funds are verifiably held by a neutral party until the legal transfer of title is registered.",
