      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, that makes more sense. So if there's a dispute, like if the inspection shows something major is wrong and the seller refuses to fix it, how does the escrow process handle that? Does the money just stay stuck in that account indefinitely?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Good question—no, the money doesn't sit in limbo forever. In Ontario, if a condition like the inspection fails and the buyer backs out, the deposit is typically returned to the buyer, but only if the condition was properly waived or unmet in writing. If the seller disputes that, the funds stay in trust until both parties agree or a court or arbitration decides.\n\nThe escrow agreement usually has a timeline—often 30 to 90 days—for resolving disputes. If no resolution happens, either party can apply to the Ontario Superior Court or use the Real Estate Council's dispute process to force a release. The brokerage holding the funds won't release them without mutual written consent or a court order, so it's not indefinite, but it can feel like it if negotiations drag on. That's why your lawyer will push for clear condition deadlines and written documentation at every step.",
      "source": "target",
